A triangle with a horizontal side and a vertical side is a right triangle whose legs are parallel to the axes. All points of $S$ have $x,y\ge 0$, so a horizontal leg of length $a$ and a vertical leg of length $b$ ($1\le a,b\le 10$) can include the origin in only three ways:
- right angle at the origin: vertices $(0,0),(a,0),(0,b)$;
- right angle at $(a,0)$: vertices $(0,0),(a,0),(a,b)$;
- right angle at $(0,b)$: vertices $(0,0),(0,b),(a,b)$.

(The fourth orientation, with the origin opposite the right angle on the hypotenuse side, would need a vertex with a negative coordinate.) Each case gives $10\cdot 10=100$ triangles, all distinct, so the total is $300$.

**Answer: 300**
